1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,751
In 1990, Poland's entire economy was worth 66 billion US dollars.

2
00:00:06,751 --> 00:00:10,025
That was about a quarter of Sweden's.

3
00:00:11,025 --> 00:00:14,221
In 2025, it crossed one trillion dollars.

4
00:00:14,221 --> 00:00:18,275
And it's now just nine billion short of Switzerland.

5
00:00:19,275 --> 00:00:24,746
Poland is just one of the stories in fifty-five years of Europe's economic race.

6
00:00:24,746 --> 00:00:26,250
Let's watch it happen.

7
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:31,798
First, what the bars show. Each one is a country's GDP:

8
00:00:31,798 --> 00:00:35,100
the value of everything it produces in a year.

9
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:40,308
It's in US dollars, at each year's prices and exchange rates,

10
00:00:40,308 --> 00:00:43,275
and every figure comes from the World Bank.

11
00:00:44,275 --> 00:00:49,635
We've included fourteen of Europe's largest economies: the big EU members,

12
00:00:49,635 --> 00:00:52,750
the United Kingdom, Switzerland and Norway.

13
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:59,678
Russia and Turkey aren't in this race. A large part of each lies outside Europe,

14
00:00:59,678 --> 00:01:04,050
and the World Bank's figures for Russia only start in 1989.

15
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:07,488
One note on Germany. Before 1991,

16
00:01:07,488 --> 00:01:15,025
the World Bank estimates the whole of today's Germany by working backwards from West Germany's growth.

17
00:01:16,025 --> 00:01:21,666
And because everything is in dollars, a bar can jump or drop when a currency moves,

18
00:01:21,666 --> 00:01:25,132
even if nothing changed at home. Keep that in mind.

19
00:01:25,132 --> 00:01:27,850
It explains several of the twists ahead.

20
00:01:29,250 --> 00:01:34,915
1970. Germany leads, with 217 billion dollars. Then France,

21
00:01:34,915 --> 00:01:37,700
the United Kingdom and Italy.

22
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:43,970
Spain is fifth, at 41 billion, a long way behind. Ireland is last,

23
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:47,802
at just four billion. And Poland isn't here yet:

24
00:01:47,802 --> 00:01:51,475
the World Bank's figures for it start in 1990.

25
00:01:52,475 --> 00:01:57,636
Through the 1970s, every bar grows fast. Some of that is real growth,

26
00:01:57,636 --> 00:02:01,450
but much of it is inflation and a weakening dollar.

27
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:08,600
By 1980, Germany has passed 950 billion dollars.

28
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:17,423
Then something strange happens. Between 1980 and 1985, almost every bar shrinks.

29
00:02:17,423 --> 00:02:21,725
Spain falls from 233 billion dollars to 181.

30
00:02:23,125 --> 00:02:26,949
Europe didn't get poorer. The dollar became very strong,

31
00:02:26,949 --> 00:02:30,295
so each country's output was worth fewer dollars.

32
00:02:30,295 --> 00:02:35,075
It's the clearest example on this chart of a currency moving the bars.

33
00:02:36,075 --> 00:02:42,475
Now watch Italy and the United Kingdom. In 1986, Italy moves ahead of Britain.

34
00:02:43,475 --> 00:02:49,350
Italians called it il sorpasso, the overtaking, and it was celebrated in Rome.

35
00:02:50,350 --> 00:02:56,075
Britain takes the place back in 1988, then loses it again a year later.

36
00:02:57,475 --> 00:03:04,051
1990. Germany is reunited. And a new name appears near the bottom of the chart:

37
00:03:04,051 --> 00:03:10,960
Poland, at 66 billion dollars, thirteenth of our fourteen. Ireland is even smaller,

38
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:12,125
at 49 billion.

39
00:03:13,525 --> 00:03:17,573
Britain finally settles ahead of Italy for good in 1994.

40
00:03:17,573 --> 00:03:19,598
Both economies were growing.

41
00:03:19,598 --> 00:03:24,875
The swaps mostly follow the exchange rate between the lira and the pound.

42
00:03:26,475 --> 00:03:32,080
In 1997, the UK passes France for second place, helped by a strong pound.

43
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:37,225
Germany stays first, as it will in every single year on this chart.

44
00:03:38,625 --> 00:03:44,972
The euro arrives in 1999, and for its first few years it's weak against the dollar.

45
00:03:44,972 --> 00:03:47,725
That holds the eurozone's bars back.

46
00:03:49,125 --> 00:03:54,879
Then it strengthens, and by 2007, Europe's bars are higher than ever. Germany,

47
00:03:54,879 --> 00:04:01,075
three and a half trillion dollars. The UK, three point one. France, two point seven.

48
00:04:03,075 --> 00:04:05,948
Spain has been booming. By 2008,

49
00:04:05,948 --> 00:04:10,617
its economy is worth one point six trillion dollars:

50
00:04:10,617 --> 00:04:13,850
almost forty times its size in 1970.

51
00:04:14,850 --> 00:04:18,695
Then the financial crisis. The pound falls hard,

52
00:04:18,695 --> 00:04:23,100
and in 2009 France takes second place back from the UK.

53
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:28,750
Spain's housing bubble bursts, and its economy shrinks for years.

54
00:04:30,150 --> 00:04:37,597
Second place changes hands again in 2012, 2013 and 2014: three swaps in three years.

55
00:04:37,597 --> 00:04:42,650
Since 2014, the UK has kept second place in dollar terms.

56
00:04:44,450 --> 00:04:50,903
2015. The euro falls sharply against the dollar. Add up all fourteen economies,

57
00:04:50,903 --> 00:04:56,294
and they shrink by about twelve percent in a single year, from 18.

58
00:04:56,294 --> 00:04:58,500
3 trillion dollars to 16.2.

59
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:02,038
Now look at Ireland. In 2015,

60
00:05:02,038 --> 00:05:08,156
its official growth was so large that economists nicknamed it leprechaun economics.

61
00:05:08,156 --> 00:05:12,137
Big international companies had moved valuable assets,

62
00:05:12,137 --> 00:05:15,675
and the profits that come with them, to Ireland.

63
00:05:16,675 --> 00:05:20,825
That's why Ireland's own government uses a different measure,

64
00:05:20,825 --> 00:05:25,791
called modified national income, when it plans its budget. On this chart,

65
00:05:25,791 --> 00:05:29,125
Ireland looks richer than it feels on the ground.

66
00:05:30,925 --> 00:05:36,482
2022. The dollar is strong again, and most bars dip. But not Norway's.

67
00:05:36,482 --> 00:05:41,325
With oil and gas prices soaring, Norway jumps to ninth place.

68
00:05:42,725 --> 00:05:49,175
When energy prices fall back, so does Norway. By 2025, it's down to thirteenth.

69
00:05:50,575 --> 00:05:56,231
And Poland. Through the 2000s and 2010s, it trades places with Belgium,

70
00:05:56,231 --> 00:06:02,125
Sweden and Norway again and again. From 2018, it stays ahead of all three.

71
00:06:03,525 --> 00:06:11,334
In 2025, Poland crosses one trillion dollars: 1.035 trillion. Switzerland is at 1.

72
00:06:11,334 --> 00:06:17,809
044 trillion. A year earlier, the gap was 52 billion. Now it's nine.

73
00:06:17,809 --> 00:06:24,475
If 2026 looks anything like 2025, Poland passes Switzerland next year.

74
00:06:25,475 --> 00:06:34,260
So here is 2025. Germany: 5.05 trillion dollars, first in every year since 1970.

75
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:43,484
The UK, 4.0 trillion. France, 3.37. Italy, 2.55. Spain, 1.91. The Netherlands, 1.33.

76
00:06:43,484 --> 00:06:48,425
Then Switzerland and Poland, almost touching.

77
00:06:50,625 --> 00:06:56,638
Three things to take away. One: the top four haven't changed in fifty-five years.

78
00:06:56,638 --> 00:07:00,275
Germany, the UK, France and Italy, in some order.

79
00:07:01,275 --> 00:07:05,249
Two: most of the swaps between them come from exchange rates,

80
00:07:05,249 --> 00:07:08,050
not from one country suddenly getting rich.

81
00:07:09,050 --> 00:07:13,672
Three: the real movers started far behind. In dollar terms,

82
00:07:13,672 --> 00:07:18,450
Spain's economy is about forty-six times bigger than in 1970,

83
00:07:18,450 --> 00:07:23,150
and Poland's is more than fifteen times bigger than in 1990.

84
00:07:24,350 --> 00:07:29,932
But dollars per country don't tell you how well people live. Next on Terrachart:

85
00:07:29,932 --> 00:07:35,235
minimum wages in every EU country, and what they really buy. And after that,

86
00:07:35,235 --> 00:07:36,700
how Poland caught up.

87
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:39,765
Sources are in the description.

88
00:07:39,765 --> 00:07:44,518
If you think we should have measured this differently, tell us in the comments.

89
00:07:44,518 --> 00:07:45,300
We read them.
